<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bastetani</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.) · capital Basti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Iberian people of the Granada highlands, whose capital Basti yielded the seated stone goddess known as the Lady of Baza.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bastetani occupied the uplands of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, from the mid-first millennium BCE. Their main town was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basti-iberia">Basti</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Baza</a>, and they bordered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oretani">Oretani</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/contestani">Contestani</a> and coastal <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bastuli">Bastuli</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in fortified towns on hills controlling routes between the coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley. Their elites were buried in rich chamber tombs, as at Tútugi (Galera) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basti-iberia">Basti</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-baza">Lady of Baza</a>, a painted stone statue of an enthroned woman used as a cinerary urn, was found in 1971. Their economy combined farming, herding and esparto working.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Carthaginian control, the Bastetani passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> during the Second Punic War and joined the great revolt of 197 BCE before being pacified. Their region was divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-baetica">Baetica</a> in Roman times.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bastetani.jpg" alt="Bastetani" width="320" height="417"><figcaption>Image: Ángel M. Felicísimo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dama_de_Baza_(HR)_(24472141522).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Basti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bastitani</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=bastetani" aria-label="Bastetani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4611 829 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bastetani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4931 1085L4975 1081M4931 1085L4881 1072"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4975 1081h0M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 1085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 1085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Basti. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=bastetani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
